{
  "text": " DPI-Net from MIT CSAIL at ICLR 2019 learns particle dynamics for rigid bodies, deformable  objects, and fluids. Its input is a set of particles, carrying positions, velocities, and  physical attributes. Nearby particles form an interaction graph, which is rebuilt as the  scene changes. Messages pass along these edges, so each particle can respond to its neighbors.  Multiple propagation steps carry effects farther through the graph before the next physical  time step. A hierarchy adds a shortcut. Particles communicate through cluster routes, spreading  information across a whole object. The learned update predicts the next particle state, and  repeated updates produce a future trajectory. For control, the system compares that predicted  outcome with a target shape, then differentiates the error back to candidate actions. After  executing an action, a new observation restarts prediction and optimization. Dynamic neighborhoods  follow motion. Repeated messages carry local effects. Hierarchy connects distant parts  of an object.",
